  "translation": {
    "literal": "Li Bai boards his boat, about to set out, when suddenly from the bank comes the sound of stamping and singing. The water of Peach Blossom Pool is a thousand feet deep, but it does not equal the affection with which Wang Lun sees me off.",
    "poetic": "Li Bai is on his boat, just pushing off, when a song with stamping feet rings out from the bank. Peach Blossom Pool is a thousand feet deep, yet shallower than the love Wang Lun shows me now."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "story",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Li Bai and Wang Lun",
      "body": "Wang Lun was a villager in Jing County (modern Anhui) who admired Li Bai and welcomed him with wine. The poem was written as a thank-you when Li Bai left, around the 750s. A well-known story, probably a later legend, says Wang Lun lured Li Bai there with a letter promising \"ten li of peach blossom and ten thousand taverns\". The place turned out to be a pool named Peach Blossom and a tavern run by someone named Wan."
    },
    {
      "id": "stamping",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "踏歌, the farewell song",
      "body": "踏歌 is a folk custom: people link arms, stamp their feet in time and sing. Wang Lun and his neighbours did not announce themselves: Li Bai only hears the sound, and the surprise is what makes the poem. The last line compares the pool's depth with the depth of feeling, and says feeling is deeper."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 行 · 聲 · 情",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 行 (xíng), 聲 (shēng) and 情 (qíng), all in the -ing / -eng rhyme. Line three ends in 尺 and does not rhyme."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "將 is read jiāng (as in 將欲, about to), and 行 is read xíng (to go, to set out). 乘 is read chéng (to ride)."
    }
  ],
